  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 301 PM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nThe consensus among ensemble clusters and deterministic models is \nthat the upper-levels across the CONUS through the workweek will be \ncharacterized by some degree of ridging in the west and troughing in \nthe east. With the Middle Mississippi Valley positioned between \nthese two features and beneath northwesterly flow aloft, our local \nweather will remain active. Confidence is high that the \naforementioned front will have cleared the CWA by Monday morning, \nthough with flow aloft in its wake being only weakly northwesterly, \nleading to meager advection of cooler air. While most of the CWA is \nexpected to see a break in the heat, with afternoon temperatures \nremaining in the 80s, portions of far southeastern Missouri still \nhave a 50-70% chance of reaching and exceeding 90 degrees per the \nNBM. \n\nThe post-frontal air mass will quickly shift eastward on Tuesday, \nreturning south-southwesterly flow and warmer air to the CWA through \nWednesday. Where 850mb temperatures are warmest along and south of I-\n70, afternoon temperatures both days have a medium chance (50-70% \nper the NBM) of reaching and exceeding 90 degrees. \n\nA series of shortwaves will move through the Midwest via the \nnorthwesterly flow Wednesday through Thursday signaled by a FROPA on \nWednesday. This FROPA will serve has the most focused chance for \nrain during the extended period, though there is large spread among \nguidance on where this rain will occur near and within the CWA and \nhow quickly the front moves through the area. While most of the area \nhas at least a low chance (20-30%) for lighter rainfall, wherever \nrainfall is able to be focused, upwards of 1-2\" can be expected. \nThis front will also mark the beginning of at least a few days of \ntemperatures through the end of the workweek and early weekend.",
